,1112],{},": a stray ",[18,1170,1171],{},"let _ = expr;"," is easy to miss in a diff — search for it specifically when a value seems to vanish without a compiler warning, since it deliberately silences \"unused\" lints while still running side effects.",[380,1174,1175,1113,1178,1180,1181,1183,1184,1186,1187,1189,1190,1193],{},[110,1176,1177],{},"Performance",[18,1179,358],{}," values are inlined at every use site (no memory location, no runtime lookup), while ",[18,1182,520],{}," values have a single fixed address — prefer ",[18,1185,358],{}," for small, cheap values and ",[18,1188,520],{}," when you need a stable address (e.g., to hand a ",[18,1191,1192],{},"&'static"," reference across an FFI boundary).",[380,1195,1196,1113,1199,1202,1203,1206,1207,1209],{},[110,1197,1198],{},"Clippy",[18,1200,1201],{},"clippy::let_and_return"," flags the ",[18,1204,1205],{},"let x = expr; x"," pattern as unnecessary — a good nudge that not every intermediate value needs its own ",[18,1208,20],{}," binding.",[14,1211,1213],{"id":1212},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[377,1215,1216,1234,1247,1258,1273,1302,1318,1328,1339,1348,1366,1385,1396,1423],{},[380,1217,1218,1113,1223,1226,1227,1229,1230,1233],{},[110,1219,1220,1221],{},"Unused ",[18,1222,116],{},[18,1224,1225],{},"warning: variable does not need to be mutable",". Fix by removing ",[18,1228,116],{}," or prefix ",[18,1231,1232],{},"_mut"," if intentional.",[380,1235,1236,1113,1239,1242,1243,1246],{},[110,1237,1238],{},"Unused variables",[18,1240,1241],{},"let _x = 5;"," (leading underscore) suppresses the warning; ",[18,1244,1245],{},"_"," itself drops the value immediately.",[380,1248,1249,1253,1254,1257],{},[110,1250,1251],{},[18,1252,1171],{}," evaluates ",[18,1255,1256],{},"expr"," then immediately drops the result — useful for side effects.",[380,1259,1260,1263,1264,1267,1268,1270,1271,189],{},[110,1261,1262],{},"Capture in closures",": a closure capturing ",[18,1265,1266],{},"x"," immutably borrows; capturing mutably requires the variable to be ",[18,1269,116],{}," and the closure itself ",[18,1272,116],{},[380,1274,1275,1278,1279,619,1282,619,1285,1288,1289,1291,1292,1294,1295,1152,1298,1152,1300,189],{},[110,1276,1277],{},"Const generics \u002F types in const",": types like ",[18,1280,1281],{},"Vec",[18,1283,1284],{},"String",[18,1286,1287],{},"Box"," can't live in ",[18,1290,358],{}," context (no heap at compile time), but they can in ",[18,1293,520],{}," only via ",[18,1296,1297],{},"lazy_static",[18,1299,1155],{},[18,1301,1144],{},[380,1303,1304,1113,1307,1310,1311,1314,1315,1317],{},[110,1305,1306],{},"Shadowing footgun",[18,1308,1309],{},"let x = something_that_panic();"," after ",[18,1312,1313],{},"let x = 5;"," — the first ",[18,1316,1266],{}," is shadowed and dropped at scope end, but the panic happens during init of the new binding.",[380,1319,1320,1323,1324,1327],{},[110,1321,1322],{},"Initialization required",": Rust has no \"uninitialized variable\" UB like C. ",[18,1325,1326],{},"let x: i32;"," followed by a read before any assignment is a compile error.",[380,1329,1330,1113,1335,1338],{},[110,1331,1332,1334],{},[18,1333,20],{}," chains (unstable)",[18,1336,1337],{},"let Some(x) = opt && x > 0"," — not stable; use explicit checks.",[380,1340,1341,1113,1344,1347],{},[110,1342,1343],{},"Tuples and unit",[18,1345,1346],{},"let () = some_fn();"," pattern-matches that the function returns unit; useful for \"I expect this to return nothing.\"",[380,1349,1350,1113,1355,1358,1359,1362,1363,1365],{},[110,1351,1352,1353],{},"Shadowing can change type but not ",[18,1354,116],{},[18,1356,1357],{},"let x = 5; let x = \"hello\";"," works (different types), but ",[18,1360,1361],{},"let mut x = 5; let x = \"hello\";"," still needs explicit ",[18,1364,20],{}," to shadow, not reassignment.",[380,1367,1368,1113,1371,1374,1375,1378,1379,1382,1383,189],{},[110,1369,1370],{},"Mutable binding ≠ mutable reference",[18,1372,1373],{},"let mut x = 5; x = 6;"," works, but ",[18,1376,1377],{},"let x = 5; x = 6;"," is a compile error. A ",[18,1380,1381],{},"&mut"," reference lets you mutate if the underlying binding is ",[18,1384,116],{},[380,1386,1387,1113,1390,1314,1393,1395],{},[110,1388,1389],{},"Drop order with shadowing",[18,1391,1392],{},"let x = String::from(\"a\"); let x = String::from(\"b\");",[18,1394,1284],{}," is dropped at the end of its scope, not at the statement boundary.",[380,1397,1398,1113,1401,1404,1405,1408,1409,1412,1413,1416,1417,1420,1421,189],{},[110,1399,1400],{},"Partial moves and shadowing",[18,1402,1403],{},"let s = String::from(\"hi\"); let s = &s;"," is a rebind (not move), ",[18,1406,1407],{},"s"," is now a reference. But ",[18,1410,1411],{},"let (a, b) = (String::from(\"hi\"), 5); let a = 10;"," shadows ",[18,1414,1415],{},"a"," only, ",[18,1418,1419],{},"b"," still holds the ",[18,1422,1284],{},[380,1424,1425,1432,1433,1435,1436,1438],{},[110,1426,1427,573,1429,1431],{},[18,1428,572],{},[18,1430,576],{}," to read\u002Fwrite",": Even reading a ",[18,1434,572],{}," is undefined behavior without synchronization; use ",[18,1437,583],{}," for thread-safe access.",[14,1440,1442],{"id":1441},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[106,1444,1445],{},"What does this print?",[23,1447,1448],{"language":25},[27,1449,1451],{"className":29,"code":1450,"language":25,"meta":31,"style":31},"struct Guard(i32);\n\nimpl Drop for Guard {\n    fn drop(&mut self) {\n        println!(\"dropping {}\", self.0);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let g = Guard(1);\n    let g = Guard(2);\n    println!(\"end of main\");\n}\n",[18,1452,1453,1467,1471,1487,1505,1527,1532,1536,1540,1551,1569,1586,1598],{"__ignoreMap":31},[35,1454,1455,1458,1461,1463,1465],{"class":37,"line":38},[35,1456,1457],{"class":41},"struct",[35,1459,1460],{"class":192}," Guard",[35,1462,243],{"class":44},[35,1464,788],{"class":192},[35,1466,498],{"class":44},[35,1468,1469],{"class":37,"line":62},[35,1470,205],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":204},[35,1472,1473,1476,1479,1482,1484],{"c